Z||000000||Avery Montembeault||08-29-2001||10:18 PM||||read an article recently about the super duty conversion kit (3 liter engine), which basically amounted to a new cylinder head and various other goodies. now, while the focus of the article was on the super duty engine, they do make mention that the tech 4/ iron duke engine could have the same modifacation, giving it over 300 hp (in theory) however they say the engine may not be able to handle that kind of repeated stress. My question is, has anyone out there tried this, or does my car have to be the guinia pig? <P>heres the article (page 1 and 2)<BR> <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/scans/form4-800-1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/scans/form4-800-1.jpg</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/scans/form4-800-2.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/scans/form4-800-2.jpg</A> <P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||08-30-2001||01:26 PM||||BrrrrruuuuwwwwwwwWWWWAAAAAAAAAAAA*BLAM*<P>The piece of junk does this quite nicely all on it's lonesome.<P>Quad 4 is a much much much better and stronger motor, and already has DOHC heads. good deal lighter too I belive.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||08-30-2001||01:50 PM||||Fisrt they used an SD4 engine in the street rod. You cannot use a regular 2.5L to achieve the same power. The block would disintigrate. The stock 2.5 is a thinner walled block. The bottom end will pull itself apart under high HP set-ups. The SD heads can mount to a stock 2.5L but it would require some machining. There was a guy around here who built up his duke many years ago with SD4 parts (cam, crank, rods, and the hi-flo head). I think he got out about 180hp. He also found out the bottom end can't hold the power. Within 6 months the lower end let loose. <BR>Either get an SD4 block or a 1977-1979 2.5L block. (The 77-79 blocks were mechanically 1/2 way between the 84 and up 2.5L and the SD4) The 77-79 blocks were heavier/stronger  and will require some work to adapt to the transverse mounts. (It was a RWD type engine)<BR>If done correctly it would get you to 200hp.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||08-30-2001||11:28 PM||||An SD4 will run you around 10 grand.  Of course, I think it would almost be worth it to have an SD4 Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
